  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A complex and initially disorganized surface \nand upper level pattern today will begin to consolidate into a \nnotable East Coast coastal low mid to late-week with a long\nduration of strong winds, very high seas, and coastal flooding\nacross the OBX and adjacent coastal waters, sounds, and rivers.\n\nCurrently, ENC remains wedged between two systems, with an upper\nlow and cold front to the north and west, while weak coastal\ntroughing off the coast. The cold front will continue to slowly\nsag south from Virginia into North Carolina and strengthen nerly\nflow over the next 12-24 hours. Periods of showers will break\nout today as vorticity advection slowly increases with fgen\nbanding aloft leading to the rounds of showers. A few storms may\ndevelop as well, though with weak instability present due to\nwidespread cloudiness, the thunder threat is minimal and\ncertainly no strong to severe expected. \n\nLate tonight into Wed morning, the above- mentioned weak low is\nthen forecast to deepen along the consolidating, and \nstrengthening, frontal/baroclinic zone off the East Coast. \n22/00Z model suite has trended quicker with pressure falls and \nresultant strengthening northeasterly winds, with gusty winds \nbecoming gales as early as early Wed morning for northern \ncoastal areas and quickly spreading south through the afternoon.\n\nConfidence continue to increase regarding a sub-1000mb low \ndeveloping offshore during this time, and this is strongly \nsupported by deterministic, ensemble, and machine learning \nguidance. Because of the blocky nature of the upper level \npattern over the Atlantic, the low will be slow moving and\nmeander about off the Mid Atlantic coast through late in the\nweek, and thus a prolonged duration of high winds on the coast\nwill lead to high impacts regarding coastal flooding on the OBX.\n\nPeriods of rain, some heavy at times, may lead to rainfall \ntotals of 2-3\" across parts of the area, though with this rain\nbeing drawn out over several days (Tuesday through Thursday),\nsignificant rainwater flooding is not expected at this time.\n\nThe strongest winds and greatest risk of coastal impacts is \nexpected Thursday into Friday, especially along the OBX. \nPersistent northeasterly winds coupled with a longer fetch \nacross the Atlantic should support long-period swell of 10-13s \nand seas of 10-16+ ft leading to breaking wave heights of over\n10 ft for beaches from Duck to Cape Lookout. Not only will this\ncreate dangerous surf conditions, but this will also present a \nrisk of beach erosion, ocean overwash at vulnerable locations, \nand coastal flooding (both soundside and oceanside), with the\npotential for significant impacts on Hatteras and Ocracoke\nIslands where a Coastal Flood Watch has been issued. Large\nbattering waves may last into the weekend even as winds diminish\ndue to the low potentially retrograding towards the Mid Atlantic\ncoastline to the north of ENC, as the AIFS and a decent handful\nof ECM ensembles indicate. The strong winds on the OBX may\neventually warrant Wind Advisory issuance on Thursday and\npotentially into Friday.",
